implementationPlan.description:
Tests first (load test-driven-development). Edit only packages/moe-daemon/src/benchmarks/workloadSnapshot.test.ts.

(a) In describe('portable paths'), BAD_PATHS: directly after the row `['CONIN$ device', 'CONIN$'],` add exactly these three rows. The middle one has TWO spaces; do not let an editor collapse them.
    ['CON device with a space before the extension', 'CON .txt'],
    ['nested aux device with spaces before the extension', 'a/aux  .c'],
    ['COM1 device with a space before the extension', 'COM1 .log'],
The existing it.each already refuses each row in every placement: initialFiles, assessmentFiles, source.paths and a direct digestFiles call. It asserts the INVALID_INPUT code and the field each time.

(b) Directly after it('accepts paths exactly at the length and depth bounds', ...), add:
  it('accepts names that only begin with a device name', () => {
    const files: Files = { 'con x': '', 'console.log': '' };
    expect(ws.digestFiles(files)).toBe(referenceDigest(files));
  });
This test guards against over-widening. It fails if the rule becomes `(?:[ .].*)?$` (refuses 'con x') or prefix-only (refuses 'console.log'). Both mutants were killed in the architect prototype.

Red proof: run it in the isolated snapshot described in step 3, never in /mnt/d. With HEAD's workloadSnapshot.ts and this test file, `npx vitest run src/benchmarks/workloadSnapshot.test.ts` must fail exactly the three new rows; the acceptance test passes. The architect prototype at HEAD 981784e gave 3 failed, 152 passed. Record the result in the step note.
